
# 📘 Git Workflow Guide for Team Members (Beginners)

## 🔹 Project Branch Structure

We are using the following Git structure:

```
main
 └── develop
      ├── eda
      ├── visualization
      └── ml
```

### What each branch means

| Branch          | Purpose                      | Who uses it        |
| --------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `main`          | Final stable code            | ❌ No one directly  |
| `develop`       | Combined work from all teams | ❌ No direct work   |
| `eda`           | Exploratory Data Analysis    | EDA team           |
| `visualization` | Charts, dashboards, plots    | Visualization team |
| `ml`            | Machine Learning models      | ML team            |

🚫 **Never work directly on `main` or `develop`**

---

## 🔹 One-Time Setup (Only Once)

### Step 1: Clone the repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/dbdagroup7/-CitiBike-Demand-Optimization
cd -CitiBike-Demand-Optimization
```

### Step 2: Check your current branch

```bash
git status
```

You will usually be on:

```
On branch main
```

That is normal.

---

## 🔹 Very Important Rule (Read This Carefully)

> ❗ You must **always pull latest changes** before starting work
> ❗ You must work **only on your assigned branch**

---

## 🔹 How to Start Work (When You Are on `main`)

### Step 1: Switch to your assigned branch

#### If you are in **EDA team**

```bash
git checkout eda
```

#### If you are in **Visualization team**

```bash
git checkout visualization
```

#### If you are in **ML team**

```bash
git checkout ml
```

---

### Step 2: Pull latest changes (MANDATORY)

```bash
git pull origin <your-branch-name>
```

Examples:

```bash
git pull origin eda
git pull origin visualization
git pull origin ml
```

✅ This ensures you are working on **latest code**

---

## 🔹 How to Do Your Work

1. Make changes to files (code, notebooks, etc.)
2. Save your files

---

## 🔹 How to Push Your Work (Step-by-Step)

### Step 1: Check what you changed

```bash
git status
```

---

### Step 2: Add changes

```bash
git add .
```

---

### Step 3: Commit your changes

```bash
git commit -m "EDA: added missing value analysis"
```

📌 Examples of good commit messages:

* `EDA: initial data cleaning`
* `Visualization: added demand trend plots`
* `ML: baseline regression model`

---

### Step 4: Pull again (VERY IMPORTANT)

```bash
git pull origin <your-branch-name>
```

Why?
👉 In case someone else pushed changes while you were working.

---

### Step 5: Push your changes

```bash
git push origin <your-branch-name>
```

Examples:

```bash
git push origin eda
git push origin visualization
git push origin ml
```

---

## 🔹 How Your Work Gets into `develop`

🚫 **DO NOT MERGE LOCALLY**

Instead:

1. Go to **GitHub website**
2. Click **Compare & Pull Request**
3. Base branch: `develop`
4. Compare branch: your branch (`eda` / `ml` / `visualization`)
5. Create Pull Request

Team lead will review and merge.

---

## 🔹 Daily Workflow (MEMORIZE THIS)

Every day, follow this order 👇

```bash
git checkout <your-branch>
git pull origin <your-branch>

# do work

git add .
git commit -m "message"
git pull origin <your-branch>
git push origin <your-branch>
```

---

## 🔹 Common Mistakes (Avoid These 🚫)

❌ Working on `main`
❌ Pushing directly to `develop`
❌ Skipping `git pull`
❌ Using someone else’s branch
❌ Large commits without messages

---

## 🔹 If You See Errors

### ❓ “Branch already exists”

👉 Just switch to it:

```bash
git checkout branch-name
```

---

### ❓ Merge conflict

👉 Stop and **inform the team lead**

---

## 🔹 Golden Rules (Final)

1. **One branch = one responsibility**
2. **Pull before push**
3. **Never touch `main`**
4. **Use Pull Requests**
5. **Ask if unsure**

---

## 🧠 Simple Memory Trick

> **Checkout → Pull → Work → Add → Commit → Pull → Push**

---


